  "preamble": "Spirantization of the voiceless bilabial plosive [p] to the voiceless bilabial fricative [ɸ] keeps the place of articulation but releases the closure into friction. It is the usual first step in the weakening chain p > ɸ > h > ∅: Japanese word-initial /p/ became [ɸ] (still heard in fu [ɸɯ]) before most instances went on to [h]; Proto-Celtic turned Indo-European *p into *ɸ before losing it; and in Venda (Bantu) Proto-Bantu *p surfaces as the bilabial fricative written fh. In Cuzco Quechua, /p/ is realized [ɸ] in the syllable coda (/ˈʎiptʼa/ [ˈʎiɸtʼa]). In the Chibchan family, Térraba changed Proto-Chibchan *p to ɸ except between vowels, and Guatuso (Maléku) changed it everywhere except word-initially.",
